$\begin{align}
y&=\tan(x)
\\\\
\dfrac{dy}{dt}&=\sec^2(x)\cdot\dfrac{dx}{dt}
\end{align}$

We are given that $\dfrac{dy}{dt}=-6$, and we want to find $\dfrac{dx}{dt}$ when $x=-\dfrac{\pi}{3}$.

Let's plug $\blueD{x=-\dfrac{\pi}{3}}$ and $\goldD{\dfrac{dy}{dt}=-6}$ into the equation we obtained:

$\begin{align}
\goldD{\dfrac{dy}{dt}}&=\sec^2(\blueD{x})\cdot\dfrac{dx}{dt}
\\\\
\goldD{-6}&=\sec^2\!\left(\blueD{-\dfrac{\pi}{3}}\right)\cdot\dfrac{dx}{dt}
\\\\
-6&=4\cdot\dfrac{dx}{dt}
\\\\
-1.5&=\dfrac{dx}{dt}
\end{align}$
